{
  "dom_id": "step-e521",
  "name": "Example: Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations",
  "problem": "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?",
  "worked_solution": "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
